															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/endren" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3785003); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3785003&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606447308" alt="Endren" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3785003)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/endren" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3785003); return false;" >Endren</a></span> <span class="odate time_1522371732 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Mar 2018 01:02</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3753408">
									

<p>This is a good, short SCP with an original idea, but some things are nagging me. Calling the SCP &quot;possibly extraterrestrial&quot; seems unnecessary to me. The foundation doesn't normally go around calling SCPs extraterrestrial, and there is seemingly no reason in the article as to why this SCP is specifically called that. I suggest either changing &quot;extraterrestrial&quot; to &quot;anomalous&quot; or adding some reason as to why the SCP would be called that.</p>
<p>The concept of the SCP living in hives is also bothering me. I would think the place the SCP lives would be more important to its containment than just one sentence mentioning it. You only name the word &quot;hive&quot; and don't elaborate what you mean by this at all, besides just a footnote. You say that the SCP lives in abandoned motels, but don't say anything else about it. What are these homes like? How do these homes come to be? Have foundation agents investigated one of these homes? Also, why are they called &quot;hives&quot; specifically? That's the foundation's designation for the home of these SCPs, but I don't see any reason for why they would call them &quot;hives&quot;. Are the homes similar to hives made by wasps or bees or something of the like?</p>
<p>The last thing that irks me about the article is the last part of the paragraph detailing the SCP's ability to mimic someone. The sentence &quot;While instances are disguised, they walk abnormally as compared to other bipedal organisms and maintain an odd posture&quot; doesn't sound like something a professional foundation scientist would write. I you should alter it to something like &quot;However, while instances are disguised, their gait is easily distinguishable from the typical human due to their abnormal posture.&quot; The last sentence feels like it could easily be removed, as the previous sentence makes it obvious that it would be easy to tell an SCP-3527 entity apart from a normal human.</p>

							</div>

								
					
